/*
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()

# Framing is the one thing a consent screen must never allow, so it is covered
# twice (frame-ancestors + X-Frame-Options). object-src/base-uri/form-action are
# safe to pin here: the app embeds no plugins, needs no <base>, and only ever
# submits forms to its own origin (the consent decision goes to the issuer over
# fetch, not a form POST), so these cost nothing and shrink the injection surface.
#
# The build appends script-src and connect-src to the policy above
# (`contentSecurityPolicy` in vite.config.ts): script-src 'self', a sha256 hash
# for each inline script in the built index.html, and Turnstile's host when the
# build carries a sitekey; connect-src 'self' and the issuer the build calls
# (VITE_OSN_ISSUER_URL). The issuer differs per tier and the hashes follow the
# HTML, so neither is written here, and the build fails if this line already
# sets either directive.

# Apple requires the App Site Association file to be served as JSON, and the
# file has no extension, so Pages would otherwise guess and send it as
# application/octet-stream. The global `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` above
# turns that guess into a hard failure rather than a lucky sniff, and the only
# symptom is that native passkeys quietly never work — the association just
# never forms. Pin the type here.
/.well-known/apple-app-site-association
  Content-Type: application/json
